/*
 * BEMS AIoT - HYBRID HARDWARE + SIMULATION FIRMWARE
 * ESP32-S3 + one physical PZEM-004T v3.0 + nineteen virtual rooms.
 *
 * DATA DESIGN
 *   Room 01: actual values read from one PZEM-004T v3.0 (UART TTL / Modbus RTU).
 *   Room 02..20: explicitly simulated values generated by the ESP32-S3.
 *   JSON contains simulated:false only for Room 01 and true for Rooms 02..20.
 *
 * IMPORTANT
 *   - There is no RS485/MAX485 and no scan of 20 PZEM addresses in this build.
 *   - There is no physical relay output. MQTT Demand Response only changes the
 *     values of simulated rooms. It never switches an electrical load.
 *   - This sketch only handles the PZEM low-voltage UART data interface. Any
 *     mains-side installation must be handled and checked by a qualified
 *     electrical professional. Do not use this sketch as a mains wiring guide.
 *
 * PIN SELECTION
 *   PZEM TX -> ESP32-S3 GPIO16 (UART RX)
 *   PZEM RX -> ESP32-S3 GPIO17 (UART TX)
 *   PZEM GND <-> ESP32-S3 GND
 *   ESP32-S3 GPIOs are 3.3V logic and are not 5V tolerant. Verify signal levels
 *   and use suitable level translation where required.
 *
 * LIBRARIES (Arduino Library Manager)
 *   - PZEM004Tv30 by Jakub Mandula
 *   - WiFiManager by tzapu
 *   - PubSubClient by Nick O'Leary
 *   - ArduinoJson 6.x by Benoit Blanchon
 *
 * BOARD PACKAGE
 *   - Espressif ESP32 Arduino core 2.x or 3.x
 *
 * MQTT SECURITY
 *   - Local broker: Mosquitto on the Ubuntu Server, TCP port 1883.
 *   - ESP32 user: bems_esp32 (password entered in the provisioning portal).
 *   - Python backend user: bems_backend (configured on the server).
 *   - Anonymous MQTT access is disabled on the broker.
 *   - This local 1883 connection is not encrypted. Keep it on a trusted LAN;
 *     do not expose port 1883 directly to the public Internet. Use a VPN or
 *     configure a properly validated TLS listener for untrusted networks.
 *   - Optional Cloud MQTT stays disabled until a real hostname and Root CA are
 *     configured below.
 */

